<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Batch 255 (28 August 2026): the Watch target's first-ever physical Info.plist —
	     see WATCH_STANDALONE_PROPOSAL_2026-08-28-ADDENDUM.md §12 for why
	     GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE had to go from YES to NO rather than keep the
	     array-typed INFOPLIST_KEY_UIBackgroundModes build-setting approach: a bare
	     single-token value (no whitespace to split on, unlike
	     INFOPLIST_KEY_UISupportedInterfaceOrientations) could not be confirmed to
	     synthesize as an <array> rather than a scalar <string> from the project file
	     alone, and the live device dump confirmed the latter. Every key below either
	     migrates a value GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = YES was already producing
	     correctly (the first four), or replaces a value Xcode was synthesizing
	     silently that a hand-written plist does not get for free (the rest) — per the
	     live `plutil -p` dump taken against the previously-generated bundle. -->

	<!-- Batch 402: Xcode's generated plist carried this, the hand-written one never
	     did. With English compiled into the binary and zh-Hans as the only .lproj,
	     a missing development region made watchOS fall back to Chinese for every
	     non-Chinese user. -->
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>

	<!-- Migrated: already correct under GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = YES. -->
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>ShelfHead</string>
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
	</array>
	<key>WKCompanionAppBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>com.justin.ShelfHead</string>

	<!-- Stage 7 (28 August 2026, WATCH_STANDALONE_PROPOSAL_2026-08-28-ADDENDUM.md
	     §5's revised stage table row 7): confirmed compatible with
	     WKCompanionAppBundleIdentifier via Apple's own primary documentation, not a
	     forum summary — Apple's doc for this key states plainly that Xcode sets it to
	     YES by default for exactly this project's own template (iOS App with Watch
	     App) and that "Users can choose to install the iOS app, the watchOS app, or
	     both." Kept alongside WKCompanionAppBundleIdentifier rather than replacing it
	     — the two are explicitly meant to coexist for this template, per that same
	     documentation. -->
	<key>WKRunsIndependentlyOfCompanionApp</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- The actual fix: hand-written as a real array, not a build-setting value that
	     could silently resolve to a scalar. This is the one key GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE
	     = YES could not be confirmed to produce correctly — see comment above. -->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>audio</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Real on-device bug fix (29 August 2026): Standalone mode showed "Retry" /
	     couldn't-reach-server on every request even with the Watch confirmed connected
	     to its own Wi-Fi — because this file had no NSAppTransportSecurity override at
	     all. The main app's Info.plist (`ShelfHead/Info.plist`) has carried this exact
	     block since before the Watch target existed (ShelfHead is a client for the
	     user's OWN self-hosted Audiobookshelf server, routinely reached over plain HTTP
	     at a LAN/VPN address the app can't know in advance — see that file's own longer
	     justification comment), but this Watch target's Info.plist was hand-written
	     from scratch in Batch 255 under GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = NO and this key was
	     simply never carried over. Under watchOS's default ATS, the Watch's own
	     independent network stack was rejecting every plain-HTTP request outright
	     before it was even sent — while the phone, which does have this exception,
	     worked fine reaching the exact same server. This is why standalone mode failed
	     specifically, and only, on the Watch's own connection:
	     `WatchStore.resolveMode()`/`bootstrapAuth()` only route through the Watch's own
	     `URLSession` when the phone isn't in the loop at all.

	     Corrected 9 September 2026, two things:

	     1. NSAllowsLocalNetworking removed. Apple documents that
	        NSAllowsArbitraryLoads is IGNORED whenever NSAllowsLocalNetworking (or the
	        WebContent/Media variants) is present in the same dictionary, so with both
	        keys set only the local-networking exemption was live and a public
	        http:// host still failed with -1022. The blanket key alone is a superset
	        of what NSAllowsLocalNetworking granted, so the LAN/VPN case this block
	        was added for keeps working. The phone's Info.plist got the same change in
	        the same pass.

	     2. The sentence above used to read "every plain-HTTP (or invalid-cert HTTPS)
	        request". The parenthetical was wrong and has been deleted: ATS exceptions
	        do not relax certificate trust. A self-signed or otherwise untrusted HTTPS
	        certificate is rejected by the system's trust evaluation, which no key in
	        this dictionary affects (and the Watch installs no URLSession trust-override
	        delegate). Such a server must be reached over plain http://, or have its CA
	        installed and trusted on the Watch.

	     Honest scope note: unlike the phone, the Watch has NO in-app plain-HTTP
	     acknowledgement. `AuthViewModel.requiresInsecureConnectionConfirmation` and the
	     URL normalisation behind it are iOS-target-only; `WatchManualSignInView` /
	     `WatchAudiobookshelfAPI.login` string-build the URL and send credentials with
	     no warning, so on the Watch this blanket exception currently has no
	     application-level gate in front of it. That gap is tracked separately as
	     REVIEW-2026-09-09.md's HIGH "Watch standalone sign-in has no URL validation and
	     no plain-HTTP gate" (fix-plan item 8) — it is not fixed here, and removing
	     NSAllowsLocalNetworking does not make it worse (the exception was already
	     blanket on paper; cleartext to a LAN host was already permitted, which is where
	     a Watch typically signs in). -->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>

	<!-- Previously synthesized silently by GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = YES; written out
	     explicitly now that nothing does that automatically. Values use build-setting
	     variables (not literals) so the existing 12-config sync rule (version bumps
	     via `sed` across every config) keeps working unchanged, and so this file
	     doesn't drift from project.pbxproj's own values the way a hardcoded copy
	     eventually would. -->
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>MinimumOSVersion</key>
	<string>$(WATCHOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET)</string>
	<!-- Build-fix (28 August 2026, real Xcode warning): "User supplied UIDeviceFamily
	     key in the Info.plist will be overwritten. Please use the build setting
	     TARGETED_DEVICE_FAMILY and remove UIDeviceFamily from your Info.plist." This
	     key was migrated here on the (wrong) assumption that GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE
	     = YES had been synthesizing it from nowhere, when it's actually driven by the
	     TARGETED_DEVICE_FAMILY build setting — already `= 4` on both Watch configs
	     (project.pbxproj), unrelated to and unaffected by this file's own
	     GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = NO / INFOPLIST_FILE switch from Batch 255. Removed
	     rather than left in as a harmless duplicate: Xcode's own warning states it
	     gets overwritten anyway, so keeping it here was never doing anything besides
	     risking exactly this warning (and, per Apple's own wording, worse if the two
	     ever disagreed). -->
	<key>WKApplication</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Corrected against a real `plutil -p` dump (28 August 2026) — the previously
	     generated bundle's actual Info.plist had no top-level CFBundleIconName at
	     all, only the nested CFBundleIcons → CFBundlePrimaryIcon → CFBundleIconName
	     shown below. The top-level key this file used to also carry was written
	     from general knowledge of Xcode's standard synthesis shape (flagged
	     `[device check]` at the time, in Batch 255), and that guess turned out to
	     be wrong in this one specific respect — removed rather than left in
	     alongside the confirmed-correct nested version. `ASSETCATALOG_COMPILER_APPICON_NAME
	     = AppIcon` (already set on both Watch configs) is what actually supplies
	     the icon regardless of which of these keys is present. -->
	<key>CFBundleIcons</key>
	<dict>
		<key>CFBundlePrimaryIcon</key>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleIconName</key>
			<string>AppIcon</string>
		</dict>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
